  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nClouds and a few light showers over northwest Missouri this \nmorning from a decaying MCS over Nebraska crossing over into our\narea. Northerly winds are advecting in drier, milder conditions  \nmaking it feel very pleasant outside. Looking at the broader\nupper-level pattern, ridging over the northern Rockies and\nInter-Mountain West has led to northwesterly flow over the\nNorthern Plains into eastern Kansas and Missouri today.\nShortwaves riding down the east side of this ridge will lead to\na fairly active pattern through the end of the week. \n\nToday, expect light showers to stay confined to northwest\nMissouri near and north of St Joe. Many echoes on radar may not\nreach the ground due to fairly dry air in the boundary layer\ntoward the surface. Temperatures will stay seasonably mild this\nafternoon with highs in upper 70s north to mid 80s across our\nfar southern counties. May still see some areas with higher\nhumidity, mainly near agricultural fields, leading to slightly\nhigher heat indices. The greater impact today is from smoke as\nthe jet pulls smoke from wildfires across the Pacific Northwest\nand northwest Canada into the central US leading to mostly just\nhazy skies. Could see air quality degrade again overnight \ntonight as the nocturnal inversion sets up potentially pulling \nsome of that smoke down toward the surface. \n\nAnother shortwave riding down the east side of the persistent\nridge to our west will develop showers and storms over south-\ncentral Nebraska and north-central Kansas later tonight bringing\nmostly just showers into our area Thursday morning. The CAMs are\nin fairly good agreement in coverage and timing of these showers\nwith soundings showing almost no instability as they arrive\naround 7-8AM Thursday morning. These do look likely to linger\nthrough the morning into at least the early afternoon, so have\nbrought down temperatures a few degrees from earlier forecasts.\nDepending on how long cloud cover and showers linger, we could\nstill be too warm. Rainfall amounts on Thursday should be mostly\nlight, around 0.25\" or less. \n\nThursday night into Friday we'll see a more substantial\nshortwave move in, aiding in the amplification of the low-level\njet oriented across eastern Kansas into western Missouri. This\nwill advect in a greater amount of moisture as this system\narrives with PWATs increasing to around 2\" by Friday morning,\nincreasing to around 2.4\" by Friday afternoon. Both these values\nare significantly higher than even the 90th percentile for \nclimatology indicating rainfall from this system will be very \nefficient with widespread rainfall totals around 1.5 to 2 inches\nlikely across much of northern Missouri Thursday night into \nFriday. The NBM shows a swath of 50-60% chance for rainfall \namounts in excess of 2 inches from just north of St Joe along \nand north of highway 36. WPC has most of our area under a \n\"Slight Risk\" (level 2 of 4) for excessive rainfall Thursday \nnight into Friday.\n\nGoing into the weekend, temperatures will start to climb once\nmore as southwesterly flow continues across our area with the\nwestern ridge shifting eastward building into the Central\nPlains. Expect a return of very hot and humid conditions with\nheat indices getting back above 100 degrees starting Saturday \nacross our southern counties. This will spread across our entire\narea on Sunday with heat indices of 100 to 115 possible Sunday\nand Monday. I guess we are getting close to August... Monday\nnight into Tuesday a nocturnal MCS may bring some relief with \nthe return of northwesterly flow.",
